    "248番目の元号です。万葉集の梅花の歌の序文「初春の令月にして、気淑く風和ぎ」から採られ、中国の古典ではなく日本の書物を典拠とした初めての元号になりました。\n\n上皇の生前退位にともなう改元で、天皇の崩御によらない改元は約200年ぶりのことです。そのため事前に日付が決まっており、多くの人がテレビで新元号の発表を見守りました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The 248th era name. It was taken from the preface to the plum-blossom poems in the Man'yōshū — “in the auspicious month of early spring, the air is fine and the wind gentle” — making it the first era name drawn from a Japanese text rather than a Chinese classic.\n\nThe change came from the abdication of the emperor rather than his death, the first such transition in about 200 years. Because the date was therefore known in advance, the whole country watched the new name being unveiled live on television."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "3年ほどの短い元号です。人形浄瑠璃「仮名手本忠臣蔵」が初演されました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A short era of about three years. The puppet play Kanadehon Chūshingura — the classic tale of the forty-seven rōnin — was first performed."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "3年ほどの短い元号です。伊能忠敬が全国の測量を進めていた時期にあたります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A short era of about three years, during which Inō Tadataka was surveying the country to make the first accurate maps of Japan."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "3年ほどの短い元号です。関東を襲った大水害（寛保の洪水）が記録されています。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A short era of about three years. The Kanpō floods, which devastated the Kantō region, are recorded here."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "60年に一度めぐる、火の陽が重なる年です。「丙午の年に生まれた女性は気が強く夫の命を縮める」という江戸時代からの迷信があり、人口統計にはっきりと跡を残しています。\n\n直近の丙午である1966年（昭和41年）は、出生数が前年より25パーセントあまりも落ち込みました。迷信が国の統計を動かした例として知られています。次の丙午は2026年（令和8年）です。\n\n十干十二支の組み合わせは60年ごとに巡ってきます。生まれた年の干支に還る数え61歳を「還暦」と呼ぶのはこのためです。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A year in which yang fire falls on yang fire, coming round once every sixty years. A superstition dating from the Edo period holds that a woman born in a hinoeuma year will be headstrong and shorten her husband's life — and it has left a visible mark on Japan's population statistics.\n\nIn 1966 (Shōwa 41), the most recent hinoeuma, births fell by more than 25 percent from the previous year. It is a well-known case of a superstition moving a national statistic. The next hinoeuma is 2026 (Reiwa 8).\n\nStem-branch pairings come round every sixty years, which is why the 61st year by the traditional count, returning to the stem-branch of your birth, is called kanreki."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「%@」を削除します。この操作は取り消せません。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "“%@” will be deleted. This cannot be undone."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「傘」の略字が「八十」に見えることから。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The abbreviated form of 傘 looks like 八十 — eighty."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「先んずれば即ち勝つ」の意味で、午前が吉、午後が凶とされます。急ぎの用事や訴訟ごとを午前中に済ませるとよい日とされます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "From the saying “act first and you win.” The morning is lucky and the afternoon is not, so this is the day to settle urgent business or legal matters before noon."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「先んずれば即ち負ける」の意味で、午前が凶、午後が吉とされます。急がず、静かに過ごすのがよい日とされ、勝負事や急用は避けられます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "From the saying “act first and you lose” — the mirror of sensho. The morning is unlucky and the afternoon is lucky. It is a day to stay calm and unhurried, avoiding contests and urgent business."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「内外、天地とも平和が達成される」という願いを込め、史記と書経から採られました。\n\n昭和天皇の崩御による改元で、元年はわずか7日間しかありません。バブル経済の崩壊に始まり、阪神・淡路大震災、東日本大震災を経験した時代で、携帯電話とインターネットが暮らしを大きく変えた時期にあたります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Taken from the Records of the Grand Historian and the Book of Documents, expressing the wish that “peace be achieved both at home and abroad, in heaven and on earth.”\n\nThe era began on the death of the Shōwa emperor, so its first year lasted only seven days. It opened with the collapse of the bubble economy and saw the Kobe and Tōhoku earthquakes, as well as the years in which mobile phones and the internet reshaped daily life in Japan."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「半」の字を分けると「八十一」になることから。将棋盤の目が81あることから盤寿とも呼ばれます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Splitting 半 apart gives 八十一 — eighty-one. It is also called banju, after the 81 squares of a shōgi board."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「卒」の略字「卆」が「九十」と読めることから。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The abbreviated form of 卒, written 卆, reads as 九十 — ninety."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「友を引く」と読まれ、葬儀を避ける日として広く知られています。友引の日は休業する火葬場も多くあります。一方で幸せを引き寄せるとして、結婚式や引き出物には好まれます。朝夕が吉、正午だけが凶とされます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Read as “pulling friends along,” this is widely known as the day to avoid funerals — the fear being that the deceased draws a friend with them. Many crematoriums in Japan close on tomobiki. For the same reason it is favoured for weddings and gifts, since it is also said to pull happiness along. Morning and evening are lucky; only midday is not."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「喜」の草書体が「七十七」と読める形になることから。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The cursive form of 喜 can be read as 七十七 — seventy-seven."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「百」の字から一画を取ると「白」になることから。百引く一で99歳を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Removing one stroke from 百 (hundred) leaves 白 — a hundred minus one, ninety-nine."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「皇」を分けると「白」（99）と「十」「一」になり、合わせて111になることから。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Splitting 皇 gives 白 (99) plus 十 and 一 — one hundred and eleven in total."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「米」の字を分けると「八十八」になることから。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Splitting 米 apart gives 八十八 — eighty-eight."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「緑」を「ろく」と読み、六十六の「六」を重ねたもの。2002年に提唱された新しい賀寿です。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Reading 緑 as roku and doubling the roku of sixty-six. A recent addition, proposed in 2002."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「茶」の字の草冠が「十十」で二十、下が「八十八」となり、合わせて108になることから。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The grass radical atop 茶 reads as 十十, twenty, and the lower part as 八十八, eighty-eight — together one hundred and eight."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "「誕生日」は人の生年月日を登録し、一覧にその方の年齢を表示します。\n\n「記念日」は結婚記念日など、年ごとに区切りを数えたい日を登録します。一覧には年齢の代わりに、その年で何回目（何周年）にあたるかを表示します。月日は経過に関係なく、その年のうちは同じ周年数のままです。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "“Birthday” registers a person's date of birth, and the list shows their age for each year.\n\n“Anniversary” registers a date you want to count in yearly milestones — a wedding anniversary, for example. Instead of an age, the list shows which anniversary falls in that year. The count doesn't depend on whether the date has passed yet — it stays the same for the whole year."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "ここに自分の生年月日を登録すると、「自分」の一覧で各年に自分が何歳になるかが表示されます。生まれた年には目印が付きます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Save your date of birth here and the Myself list will show how old you are in each year. Your birth year is marked."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "この年から数え始めます": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The count starts this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "この年で%lld周年": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "%lld years this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "この年に生まれました": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Born this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "この年の元日で数え%lld歳\nこの年の誕生日で満%lld歳": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "%lld by the traditional count from New Year's Day\n%lld on the birthday that year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "この年の誕生日で満%lld歳": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Turns %lld on the birthday this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "この解説は一般に伝えられている説明にもとづくものです。暦の解釈や由来には諸説あります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "This explanation follows the commonly given account. Interpretations and origins vary between sources."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "その年のカレンダーです。日付の下に六曜と月の満ち欠けを添えています。\n\n【六曜】\n先勝・友引・先負・仏滅・大安・赤口の6日周期で、旧暦の月日から求めます。日取りを選ぶときに探すことの多い大安は赤色で示します。\n\n【月の満ち欠け】\nパーセントは輝面率といい、月の光っている部分の面積の割合です。新月が0％、満月が100％になります。\n\n夕方から夜にかけて実際に空へ出ている月に合わせて計算しているので、その晩に見上げる月の姿とおおよそ一致します。満月（100％）は黄色で示します。\n\n旧暦をもとにした概算のため、実際の満月と1日ずれることがあります。\n\n【改元】\n改元があった年は、元号が変わる日に区切り線と新しい元号を表示します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The calendar for that year. Under each date you will find the rokuyo and the phase of the moon.\n\n[六曜 rokuyo]\nA six-day cycle — 先勝 senshō, 友引 tomobiki, 先負 senbu, 仏滅 butsumetsu, 大安 taian, 赤口 shakkō — derived from the lunisolar calendar. 大安, the day most often sought when choosing a date, is marked in red.\n\n[The moon]\nThe percentage is the illuminated fraction: how much of the moon's face is lit. A new moon is 0% and a full moon is 100%.\n\nIt is calculated for the moon as it actually stands in the sky from evening into night, so it matches roughly what you would see looking up that night. A full moon (100%) is marked in yellow.\n\nBecause it is an approximation based on the lunisolar calendar, it can be a day off from the true full moon.\n\n[Era changes]\nIn a year when the era changed, the day it changed is marked with a rule and the new era name."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "ねずみ": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Rat"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "アプリ内の文字の大きさを決めます。自動を選ぶと、iPhoneの「設定」→「画面表示と明るさ」→「テキストサイズ」に合わせて変わります。標準・大・特大を選ぶと、端末の設定に関わらずその大きさになります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Sets the text size inside the app. Auto follows iPhone Settings → Display & Brightness → Text Size. Standard, Large, and Extra Large fix the size regardless of your device setting."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "オンにすると、年に書いたメモは「自分」の一覧でだけ表示されます。年齢や名簿の一覧ではメモが隠れるため、他の人に画面を見せるときに便利です。オフにするとどの一覧でもメモが表示されます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "When on, the notes you write on a year appear only in the Myself list. They stay hidden in the Age and Roster lists, which is useful when showing your screen to someone else. When off, notes appear in every list."
          }
        }
      }
    },
