          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Birth year of someone who became %lld by the traditional count this New Year\nand turns %lld on their birthday this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "今年の記念日で%@周年です": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "%@ years as of this year's anniversary"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "今年の誕生日で満%@歳になりました": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Turned %@ on this year's birthday"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "今年の誕生日で満%@歳になります\n今日は誕生日前なので満%@歳です": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Turning %@ on this year's birthday\nStill %@ until then"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "今年の誕生日で満%@歳になる方の誕生年です": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Birth year of someone turning %@ on their birthday this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "今年の誕生日で満%lld歳になる方の誕生年です": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Birth year of someone turning %lld on their birthday this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "今年生まれた方の誕生年": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Birth year of someone born this year"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "仏も滅する": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Literally “even the Buddha perishes”"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "保存": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Save"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "元号は、天皇の即位や社会の節目に付けられる年の名前です。西暦645年の「大化」に始まり、令和で248番目にあたります。年は「令和8年」のように元号と数字で数え、元号が変わるとまた1年（元年）に戻ります。\n\n改元のあった年は、1年の途中で元号が入れ替わります。この一覧では、そうした年に2つの元号を併記しています。\n\n中国から伝わった仕組みですが、いまも国として使い続けているのは日本だけです。運転免許証、戸籍、確定申告の書類など、日本の公的な書類の多くは今も元号で年を書きます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "An era name is a name given to a span of years, marking an emperor's accession or a turning point in national life. The practice began with Taika in 645, and Reiwa is the 248th. Years are counted as “Reiwa 8,” and when the era changes the count returns to 1 — the “first year,” written 元年 rather than 1.\n\nIn a year when the era changed, two era names apply to different parts of the same year. This list shows both for such years.\n\nThe system came from China, but Japan is now the only country still using it. Driving licences, family registers, tax filings and most other official Japanese documents still write the year this way."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "元号は、天皇の即位や社会の節目に付けられる年の名前です。西暦645年の「大化」に始まり、令和はその248番目にあたります（南北朝時代に並び立った元号をどう数えるかで異説があります）。年は「令和8年」のように元号と数字で数え、元号が変わるとまた1年（元年）に戻ります。\n\n改元のあった年は、1年の途中で元号が入れ替わります。この一覧では、そうした年に2つの元号を併記しています。\n\n中国から伝わった仕組みですが、いまも国として使い続けているのは日本だけです。運転免許証、戸籍、確定申告の書類など、日本の公的な書類の多くは今も元号で年を書きます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "An era name is a name given to a span of years, marking an emperor's accession or a turning point in national life. The practice began with Taika in 645, and Reiwa is the 248th — though the total depends on how the rival era names of the Nanboku-chō period are counted. Years are counted as “Reiwa 8,” and when the era changes the count returns to 1 — the “first year,” written 元年 rather than 1.\n\nIn a year when the era changed, two era names apply to different parts of the same year. This list shows both for such years.\n\nThe system came from China, but Japan is now the only country still using it. Driving licences, family registers, tax filings and most other official Japanese documents still write the year this way."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "元年（%@月%@日〜）": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": " 1 (from %@/%@)"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "先んずれば勝ち": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Literally “first wins”"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "先んずれば負け": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Literally “first loses”"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "兎": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Rabbit"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "入学・卒業": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "shouldTranslate": false
    },
    "六曜でもっとも凶とされる日です。「仏も滅する」と書き、一日を通して凶とされるため、結婚式や開店などの祝い事は避けられます。逆に法事は差し支えないとされ、結婚式場では割引されることもあります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The most inauspicious day of the cycle. Written with characters meaning “even the Buddha perishes,” it is unlucky all day, so weddings and shop openings are avoided. Buddhist memorial services, however, are considered perfectly fine, and wedding venues often discount their rates on this day."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "六曜でもっとも縁起のよい日とされます。一日を通して吉で、結婚式や入籍、開店、契約など、めでたい事を始める日に選ばれます。結婚式場の料金が大安の日だけ高いこともあります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The most auspicious day of the rokuyo cycle. It is considered lucky from morning to night, so it is the day people pick to begin something happy — a wedding, registering a marriage, opening a shop, signing a contract. Japanese wedding venues often charge more on a taian than on any other day."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "六曜の意味を見る": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Show what this rokuyo means"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "六曜は、先勝・友引・先負・仏滅・大安・赤口の6日周期で、旧暦の月日から求めます。旧暦の月が変わるたびに並びが飛ぶのは、この計算方法によるものです。\n\n日取りを選ぶときの目安として、暦や手帳にいまも広く印刷されています。結婚式は大安に、葬儀は友引を避けて、というように、人生の節目の日取りは今も六曜を見て決められることが多くあります。\n\n仏教とは関係がなく、中国から伝わった占いが江戸時代に広まったものです。迷信として公的な暦から外されたこともありましたが、慣習として根強く残っています。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Rokuyo is a six-day cycle — sensho, tomobiki, senbu, butsumetsu, taian, shakko — calculated from the month and day of the old lunisolar calendar. The sequence jumps whenever the old month turns, which is a consequence of that calculation.\n\nIt is still printed in Japanese calendars and diaries as a guide to choosing dates. Weddings are set on taian and funerals kept off tomobiki, and the timing of many of life's formal occasions is still decided this way.\n\nDespite the Buddhist look of butsumetsu, the system has no connection to Buddhism: it came from China and spread in the Edo period. It has been struck from official calendars as superstition more than once, yet survives firmly as custom."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "六曜は先勝・友引・先負・仏滅・大安・赤口の6日周期で、旧暦の月日から求めます。日取りを選ぶときの目安として、暦や手帳にいまも広く印刷されています。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Rokuyo is a six-day cycle — sensho, tomobiki, senbu, butsumetsu, taian, shakko — calculated from the month and day of the old lunisolar calendar. It is still printed in Japanese calendars and diaries today, and people consult it when choosing a date for an important occasion."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "凶の日とされ、正午前後（おおよそ11時から13時）だけが吉とされます。「赤」の字から火や刃物、血を連想させるとして、祝い事は避けられます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "An unlucky day, except for the hours around noon (roughly 11:00 to 13:00), which are considered safe. The character 赤 (“red”) is associated with fire, blades and blood, so celebrations are avoided on this day."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "初心者": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Beginner"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "初心者を選ぶと、ボタンの名前や一覧の見方といった説明が画面に表示されます。操作に慣れたら達人に切り替えると、説明が消えて一覧を広く使えます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Beginner shows explanations on screen, such as button names and how to read the list. Once you are comfortable, switch to Expert to hide them and use more of the screen for the list."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "削除": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Delete"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "削除しますか？": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Delete?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二の動物で表す12年周期": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A 12-year cycle of animals"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の10番目です。動物は鶏。時刻では午後6時ごろ、方角では西を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The tenth branch. Its animal is the rooster. It marks around 6 p.m. and due west."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の11番目です。動物は犬。時刻では午後8時ごろ、方角では西北西を表します。戌と亥の間の方角は「戌亥（いぬい）」と呼ばれます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The eleventh branch. Its animal is the dog. It marks around 8 p.m. and the west-north-west. The direction between the dog and the boar is called inui."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の2番目です。動物は牛。時刻では午前2時ごろ、方角では北北東を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The second branch. Its animal is the ox. It marks around 2 a.m. and the north-north-east."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の3番目です。動物は虎。時刻では午前4時ごろ、方角では東北東を表します。丑と寅の間の方角は「丑寅（うしとら）」と呼ばれ、鬼が出入りする鬼門として避けられてきました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The third branch. Its animal is the tiger. It marks around 4 a.m. and the east-north-east. The direction between the ox and the tiger, ushitora, is the “demon gate” through which evil was believed to pass, and has long been avoided."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の4番目です。動物は兎。時刻では午前6時ごろ、方角では東を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The fourth branch. Its animal is the rabbit. It marks around 6 a.m. and due east."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の5番目です。動物は龍で、十二支の中で唯一の想像上の生き物です。時刻では午前8時ごろ、方角では東南東を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The fifth branch. Its animal is the dragon — the only imaginary creature among the twelve. It marks around 8 a.m. and the east-south-east."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の6番目です。動物は蛇。時刻では午前10時ごろ、方角では南南東を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The sixth branch. Its animal is the snake. It marks around 10 a.m. and the south-south-east."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の7番目です。動物は馬。時刻では真昼の12時ごろ、方角では南を表します。「正午」「午前」「午後」ということばは、この午の刻を基準にしたものです。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The seventh branch. Its animal is the horse. It marks midday and due south. The Japanese words for noon (shōgo), morning (gozen) and afternoon (gogo) are all built on this horse hour."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の8番目です。動物は羊。時刻では午後2時ごろ、方角では南南西を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The eighth branch. Its animal is the sheep. It marks around 2 p.m. and the south-south-west."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の9番目です。動物は猿。時刻では午後4時ごろ、方角では西南西を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The ninth branch. Its animal is the monkey. It marks around 4 p.m. and the west-south-west."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の最初にあたります。動物はねずみ。昔の時刻では真夜中の0時ごろ、方角では北を表します。一日と方角の起点にあたる位置です。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The first of the twelve branches. Its animal is the rat. In the old system of hours it marks midnight, and in directions the north — the starting point of both the day and the compass."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支の最後にあたります。動物は猪で、中国や韓国では豚とされます。時刻では午後10時ごろ、方角では北北西を表します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The last of the twelve branches. Its animal is the boar — a pig in the Chinese and Korean zodiacs. It marks around 10 p.m. and the north-north-west."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十二支は、子・丑・寅・卯・辰・巳・午・未・申・酉・戌・亥の12種で、12年で一巡します。それぞれに動物が当てられ、年賀状や初詣の縁起物でおなじみです。\n\nもとは順序を表す記号で、動物は覚えやすくするために後から結び付けられたものです。年だけでなく、時刻（午前・午後の「午」は正午の意味）や方角（「北東」を丑寅＝鬼門と呼ぶ）を表すのにも使われてきました。\n\n初対面の人に年齢を直接たずねる代わりに、干支をたずねて年齢を推し量る習慣もあります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The twelve branches — 子 丑 寅 卯 辰 巳 午 未 申 酉 戌 亥 — run in a twelve-year cycle. Each carries an animal, familiar from New Year cards and shrine charms.\n\nThey began as plain ordinal markers; the animals were attached later as a memory aid. They mark not only years but hours (the 午 in 午前 “a.m.” and 午後 “p.m.” is the horse hour, noon) and directions (the north-east, 丑寅, is the “demon gate” to be avoided).\n\nRather than ask someone's age directly, people in Japan sometimes ask their zodiac animal and work the age out from there."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十干と十二支を組み合わせた60年周期": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A 60-year cycle of stems and branches"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十干（甲・乙・丙…の10種）と十二支（子・丑・寅…の12種）を組み合わせた60通りのうちの一つです。十干は木・火・土・金・水の五行を陰陽に分けたもので、読みの「のえ」は兄（陽）、「のと」は弟（陰）を表します。\n\nこの組み合わせは60年ごとに巡ってきます。生まれた年の干支に還る数え61歳を「還暦」と呼ぶのはこのためです。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "One of the sixty pairings of a heavenly stem (甲, 乙, 丙 …) with an earthly branch (子, 丑, 寅 …). The ten stems are the five phases — wood, fire, earth, metal, water — each split into yang and yin; the -noe in a reading marks the elder (yang) form and -noto the younger (yin).\n\nEach pairing comes round once every sixty years. That is why the 61st year by the traditional count, when you return to the stem-branch of your birth, is called kanreki."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "十返舎一九や葛飾北斎らが活躍し、江戸の出版文化が最盛期を迎えました。間宮林蔵の樺太探査もこの頃です。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Jippensha Ikku and Katsushika Hokusai were at work, and Edo's publishing culture reached its height. Mamiya Rinzō explored Sakhalin in this period."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "南北朝の並立を含めて数えると248番目にあたります。万葉集の梅花の歌の序文「初春の令月にして、気淑く風和ぎ」から採られ、中国の古典ではなく日本の書物を典拠とした初めての元号になりました。\n\n上皇の生前退位にともなう改元で、天皇の崩御によらない改元は約200年ぶりのことです。そのため事前に日付が決まっており、多くの人がテレビで新元号の発表を見守りました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Counting the rival courts of the Nanboku-chō period, it is the 248th era name. It was taken from the preface to the plum-blossom poems in the Man'yōshū — “in the auspicious month of early spring, the air is fine and the wind gentle” — making it the first era name drawn from a Japanese text rather than a Chinese classic.\n\nThe change came from the abdication of the emperor rather than his death, the first such transition in about 200 years. Because the date was therefore known in advance, the whole country watched the new name being unveiled live on television."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "厄年": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "shouldTranslate": false
    },
    "厄年の中心にあたる年で、もっとも災いに遭いやすいとされます。神社や寺で厄払いの祈祷を受ける人が多く、正月から節分にかけて社寺が賑わいます。新しい事を始めるのは避け、身を慎むのがよいとされます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The central year of the yakudoshi, when misfortune is thought most likely. Many visit a shrine or temple for a purification rite, filling them from New Year through to setsubun. Starting new ventures is avoided and restraint is advised."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "厄年は、災いに遭いやすいとされる年齢です。数え年で見るのが基本で、男性は25・42・61歳、女性は19・33・37・61歳が本厄にあたります。その前後の年も前厄・後厄として警戒します。\n\n男性42歳（「死に」）と女性33歳（「散々」）は大厄と呼ばれ、とくに重く見られます。\n\n平安時代から続く風習で、この年には神社や寺で厄払いの祈祷を受ける人が多く、正月から節分にかけて社寺が賑わいます。ちょうど心身の変わり目や、社会的な責任が重くなる年齢にあたるため、生活を見直す節目として受け止められています。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Yakudoshi are ages at which misfortune is thought to come easily. They are counted by the traditional reckoning: for men 25, 42 and 61; for women 19, 33, 37 and 61. The years either side are watched as well, as maeyaku and atoyaku.\n\nA man's 42nd year and a woman's 33rd are the “great” yakudoshi, taken most seriously — 42 can be read as shi-ni, “to death,” and 33 as sanzan, “disastrous.”\n\nThe custom dates from the Heian period. Many people visit a shrine or temple for a purification rite in these years, filling them from New Year through to setsubun in early February. Since the ages fall where health and responsibilities tend to shift, they are widely treated as a natural moment to take stock."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "厄年を表示するために使います。「未指定」を選ぶと、この方の厄年は表示されません。\n\n厄年は、人生の中で災いに遭いやすいとされる年齢です。数え年で見るのが基本で、本厄の前後1年をそれぞれ前厄・後厄と呼びます。\n\n男性は25歳・42歳・61歳、女性は19歳・33歳・37歳・61歳が本厄にあたります（いずれも数え年）。男性の42歳と女性の33歳はとくに「大厄」と呼ばれます。\n\n年齢や数え方は神社や地域によって異なる場合があります。目安としてご覧ください。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Used to show 厄年 (yakudoshi) unlucky years. Choose Unspecified and no unlucky years are shown for this person.\n\n厄年 are ages at which misfortune is traditionally thought more likely. They are reckoned in 数え年 (kazoedoshi), and the years either side of the main one are called 前厄 (maeyaku) and 後厄 (atoyaku).\n\nFor men the main years are 25, 42, and 61; for women 19, 33, 37, and 61 — all in kazoedoshi. Men's 42 and women's 33 are known as 大厄 (taiyaku), the great unlucky years.\n\nThe ages and the way they are counted vary between shrines and regions. Treat these as a rough guide."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "友を引く": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Literally “pulls friends along”"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "取扱説明": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "User Guide"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名前": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Name"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名称": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Title"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名簿": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Roster"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名簿が空です。右上の＋から追加してください。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "No one saved yet. Tap + at the top right to add someone."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名簿で選んだ方の生年月日をもとに、各年に何歳になるかを表示します。行をタップすると、その年のカレンダーが開きます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Shows how old the person selected from the roster is in each year. Tap a row to open that year's calendar."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名簿で選んだ方の生年月日をもとに、各年に何歳になるかを表示します。行をタップすると、その年のカレンダーとメモが開きます。メモにはその年の出来事を書き留められます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Shows how old the person selected from the roster is in each year. Tap a row to open that year's calendar and note, where you can write down what happened that year."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名簿で選んだ記念日をもとに、各年で何周年になるかを表示します。行をタップすると、その年のカレンダーが開きます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Shows which anniversary falls in each year, based on the date selected from the roster. Tap a row to open that year's calendar."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "名簿で選んだ記念日をもとに、各年で何周年になるかを表示します。行をタップすると、その年のカレンダーとメモが開きます。メモにはその年の出来事を書き留められます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Shows which anniversary falls in each year, based on the date selected from the roster. Tap a row to open that year's calendar and note, where you can write down what happened that year."
          }
        }
      }
    },
