      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Age → year of birth"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "年齢を指定すると、その年齢の方が生まれた年へ移動します。行をタップすると、その年のカレンダーが開きます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Enter an age to jump to the year someone of that age was born. Tap a row to open that year's calendar."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "年齢を指定すると、その年齢の方が生まれた年へ移動します。行をタップすると、その年のカレンダーとメモが開きます。メモにはその年の出来事を書き留められます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Enter an age to jump to the year someone of that age was born. Tap a row to open that year's calendar and note, where you can write down what happened that year."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "後桜町天皇の即位にともなって改元されました。錦絵（多色刷の浮世絵）が生まれ、鈴木春信が人気を集めました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The era name was changed on the accession of Empress Go-Sakuramachi. Full-colour woodblock printing (nishiki-e) was invented in this era, and Suzuki Harunobu became popular."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "徳川吉宗が将軍を退き、家重が継いだ時期にあたります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The era in which Tokugawa Yoshimune stepped down as shogun and was succeeded by Ieshige."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "徳川吉宗による享保の改革が行われました。倹約と新田開発で幕府財政を立て直し、目安箱の設置や小石川養生所の開設もこの時期です。享保の飢饉も起きています。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Tokugawa Yoshimune carried out the Kyōhō Reforms, restoring shogunate finances through frugality and new rice-field development. The public suggestion box and the Koishikawa clinic date from this era, as does the Kyōhō famine."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "徳川吉宗の死去にともなう時期です。暦の誤差を正した宝暦暦が施行されました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The era in which Tokugawa Yoshimune died. The Hōreki calendar was adopted to correct accumulated errors in the old one."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "性別": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Gender"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "数え%lld歳（満%lld歳）の祝い": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Celebrated at %lld by the traditional count (%lld by actual age)"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "数え年を表示する": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Show traditional age"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "数え年齢": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Traditional age"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "文字どおり百歳。紀寿（きじゅ）とも呼ばれ、一世紀を生きたことを意味します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Literally a hundred years. Also called kiju, marking a full century of life."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "文字サイズ": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Text Size"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "新井白石による正徳の治が行われ、貨幣の質を元に戻す改革が進められました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Arai Hakuseki directed the Shōtoku administration, restoring the debased currency to its former quality."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "日": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "shouldTranslate": false
    },
    "日の吉凶を表す6日周期": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A six-day cycle of lucky and unlucky days"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "日生": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "shouldTranslate": false
    },
    "明暦の大火からの江戸復興が進められた時期にあたります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The era in which Edo was rebuilt after the Meireki fire."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "易経の「大亨は以て正天の道なり」から採られました。\n\n15年と短い時代ですが、都市文化が花開き、洋装や喫茶店、活動写真が広まった時期として知られます。政党政治や普通選挙運動が盛り上がったこの風潮は「大正デモクラシー」と呼ばれます。末期の1923年に関東大震災が起きました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Taken from the I Ching: “great success comes through righteousness, the way of heaven.”\n\nThough only 15 years long, it is remembered as the period when urban culture flourished — Western dress, coffee houses and moving pictures spread through the cities. The accompanying enthusiasm for party politics and universal suffrage is known as “Taishō democracy.” The Great Kantō Earthquake struck near the end of the era, in 1923."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "易経の「聖人南面して天下を聴き、明に嚮ひて治む」から採られました。\n\nこの改元から、一人の天皇に一つの元号とする「一世一元」の制度が始まります。それ以前は、災害や吉兆のたびに改元されていました。\n\n武士の世が終わり、日本が近代国家へと大きく作り替えられた時代です。太陽暦の採用（明治5年12月3日を明治6年1月1日とした）もこの時期で、これより前の日付は旧暦である点に注意が必要です。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Taken from the I Ching: “the sage faces south and listens to the world, turning toward the light to govern.”\n\nThis transition established the rule of one era name per emperor. Before Meiji, the era name was changed whenever a disaster or an auspicious omen seemed to call for it.\n\nIt was the era in which the age of the samurai ended and Japan was rebuilt as a modern state. The switch to the solar calendar also happened here: 3 December of Meiji 5 was declared 1 January of Meiji 6. Dates before that point are given in the old lunisolar calendar, which is worth remembering when reading old documents."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "暦": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Calendar"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "書経の「百姓昭明、協和万邦」から採られ、「国民の平和と世界の共存繁栄」を願う名です。\n\n64年続いた、日本の元号でもっとも長い時代です。戦争と敗戦、そして高度経済成長という激動を含むため、今も「昭和の時代は」と一つの時代像として語られます。現在の高齢者の多くが昭和生まれで、書類の生年月日欄でもっともよく見かける元号です。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Taken from the Book of Documents, expressing a wish for “peace for the people and prosperity shared among all nations.”\n\nAt 64 years it is the longest era in Japanese history. Because it contains the war, the defeat, and the rapid economic growth that followed, “back in the Shōwa era” is still used in Japan to evoke a whole period of national life. Most elderly people alive today were born in Shōwa, and it is the era name you will meet most often in the date-of-birth field of a form."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "月": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "shouldTranslate": false
    },
    "木の兄（き のえ）": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Elder wood (yang)"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "木の弟（き のと）": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Younger wood (yin)"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "未対応のメモ形式です": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Unsupported note format"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "未対応の名簿データ形式です": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Unsupported roster format"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "未指定": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Unspecified"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "未設定": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Not set"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "本厄の前の年です。厄の兆しが現れ始める年とされ、この年から慎重に過ごすよう勧められます。厄払いを受ける人も少なくありません。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The year before the main yakudoshi. Misfortune is said to begin showing itself, and caution is advised from this year on. Many people already seek a purification rite."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "本厄の次の年です。厄が薄らいでいく年とされますが、油断せずに過ごすのがよいとされます。この年をもって厄が明けます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The year after the main yakudoshi. The danger is said to be fading, though caution is still advised. With this year the unlucky period ends."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "杜甫の詩「人生七十古来稀なり」から。昔は70歳まで生きることが稀だったことに由来します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "From a poem by Du Fu: “to live seventy years has been rare since ancient times.” Reaching 70 was once uncommon."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "松平定信による寛政の改革が行われました。倹約を求める厳しい統制で、風紀の取り締まりや出版の規制が敷かれました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Matsudaira Sadanobu carried out the Kansei Reforms — a severe programme of enforced frugality, with crackdowns on public morals and censorship of publishing."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "桜田門外の変で大老井伊直弼が暗殺された年に改元されました。1年余りで文久に改まります。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The name was changed in the year the chief minister Ii Naosuke was assassinated outside the Sakurada Gate. It lasted little more than a year before becoming Bunkyū."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "標準": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Standard"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "次": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Next"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "歳": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "shouldTranslate": false
    },
    "水の兄（みず のえ）": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Elder water (yang)"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "水の弟（みず のと）": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Younger water (yin)"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "江戸の大半を焼き、江戸城の天守も失われた明暦の大火が起きました。天守はその後再建されていません。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The Meireki fire burned most of Edo and destroyed the keep of Edo Castle. It was never rebuilt."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "江戸の水不足を解消するため、玉川上水が開かれました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The Tamagawa aqueduct was opened to relieve Edo's shortage of drinking water."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "江戸を焼いた天和の大火が起きました。八百屋お七の話はこの火事に由来します。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The Tenna fire burned through Edo. The well-known story of Oshichi, the greengrocer's daughter, comes from this blaze."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "江戸城本丸の火災を理由に改元されました。外国船の来航が相次ぎ、海防への関心が高まった時期です。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The name was changed after a fire in the main keep of Edo Castle. Foreign ships were arriving with increasing frequency, and coastal defence became a pressing concern."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "江戸時代最後の元号です。禁門の変後の混乱を理由に改元されました。慶応3年の大政奉還を経て、慶応4年9月に明治へ改元されます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The last era of the Edo period. The name was changed after the upheaval of the Kinmon incident. The shogun returned power to the emperor in Keiō 3, and in the ninth month of Keiō 4 the era became Meiji."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "浅間山の噴火と天明の大飢饉により、多数の餓死者が出ました。江戸時代でもっとも深刻な飢饉とされます。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The eruption of Mount Asama and the Tenmei famine caused mass starvation. It is considered the worst famine of the Edo period."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "渋川春海が日本人の手による初の暦法「貞享暦」を作り、それまでの中国の暦から改められました。": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Shibukawa Harumi produced the Jōkyō calendar, the first calendrical system devised by a Japanese astronomer, replacing the Chinese calendar used until then."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "満年齢": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Actual age"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "漢字（熟語）をタップすると解説を表示します": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Tap any Japanese term to see what it means"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "火の兄（ひ のえ）": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Elder fire (yang)"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "火の弟（ひ のと）": {
